SM(546,{"n":"1 Corinthians 14","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 14.mp3","d":2614.7,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.56,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[21.76,"When we get started here in just a moment, uh we'll be picking up at the beginning of St."],[27.6,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians, chapter 14."],[31.84,"And I think I may have forgotten to say this last time, but it is actually true as we speak."],[37.92,"The first podcast on 1 Corinthians with the introduction is available online."],[43.92,"So you can go back and listen to it if you want to."],[47.12,"But to get us caught up to where we left off, we started a new uh section back at the beginning of chapter 12."],[55.68,"We talked about how St."],[57.44,"Paul uses this verbal phrase in First Corinthians to separate these sort of sections where he's addressing different topics where he says, now concerning this topic."],[69.24,"So our current topic that started at the beginning of chapter 12, he said, now concerning spiritual gifts."],[76.04,"And so far in terms of spiritual gifts, he gave his analogy of the body, uh comparing that to community, the church, and how it's composed of all different parts that have all different abilities."],[90.2,"They're all necessary for the body to function as a whole."],[94.04,"He talked about uh some of those particular gifts that different people have."],[100.28,"He gave examples, as it were, and then during the last section that we read and discussed last time, uh St."],[104.52,"that Paul was making the point love, love for one another, and we talked about how he particularly defined that in terms of when one person is rejoicing, we all rejoice together."],[119.68,"When one person is suffering, then we all sorrow together."],[122.56,"This love that promotes unity for the body, for the community, that love is more important than any of these, any of these gifts."],[130.96,"And you may be the most gifted person in the world, but if you don't have love, you're not useful."],[136.72,"You're not bought, you're not able to accomplish your purpose."],[139.52,"You're not able to use any of that giftedness in a way that is helpful to the body of Christ, the church."],[146.92,"And one of his sort of his last argument right where we left off last time was to make the point that all of these gifts, regardless of how important we think our own are, in the the present day within the community in the church, within the community in Corinth, the day is coming, St."],[165.5,"Paul reminded us when Christ will return."],[167.98,"And then all of these things, whether it's being able to speak in different languages and understand different languages, whether it's having some kind of wisdom or knowledge, having a lot of education, whether it's having a lot of wealth, so you're able to help those in need, all of these things are going to go away."],[185.36,"When Christ returns."],[186.64,"All of these things, as useful and as important as they can be to serve Christ in this age, when this age ends, they're done."],[194.92,"But love continues on forever, uh, which makes love ultimately superior and the thing we should pursue more than anything else."],[204.68,"And so that's when where we're going to pick up now at the beginning of chapter 14, unless anybody had any comments or questions or allegations or dispositive arguments or anything else."],[217.56,"Okay, so St."],[218.6,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians chapter 14, verse 1."],[222.92,"Pursue love and desire spiritual gifts, but especially that you may prophesy."],[228.56,"Okay, so now he's transitioning."],[230.6,"So he sort of sums up where he was before: pursue love."],[233.97,"This is the one you should pursue most of all, and desire spiritual gifts. but especially that you may prophesy"],[241.13,"Now remember, we talked about what prophecy is in the context of Saint Paul last time."],[247.29,"It's not for telling the future, Art."],[249.63,"It's not for telling the future."],[251.47,"It's not receiving some kind of mystical revelation from God."],[255.73,"Of some new knowledge, you know, go and tell this person."],[258.69,"You know, it's not like uh in the dead zone where Christopher Walken shook somebody's hand and you know, had a vision, you know, some warning for them."],[268.45,"It's not like that."],[269.57,"We talked about how prophecy is related to by Saint Paul in chapter 12 directly to wisdom, to possessing spiritual wisdom."],[278.93,"That spiritual wisdom comes, as he's been arguing all the way through."],[283.05,"Remember, he's been talking to a lot of the people in Corinth had been pagans, they had participated in these mystery rituals that promised to give them this supernatural wisdom and all of this."],[293.05,"And Saint Paul is arguing, no, that comes from the Holy Spirit."],[296.81,"We talked about how the prophetic way of life in the Old Testament is what's continued in the monastic life in the New Covenant."],[306.89,"St. John the Forerunner, Saint John the Baptist is sort of the pivot point there from one to the other."],[312.49,"So uh when he says desire to prophesy, Saint Paul is directly talking about living that kind of lifestyle and pursuing that kind of wisdom."],[322.67,"Does that mean um does St."],[326.03,"Paul mean uh asceticism?"],[328.39,"Yes, so okay."],[329.83,"Yes. To seek to draw close to God."],[333.21,"So number one is love."],[335.57,"He's saying, but then also seek out God's gifts in your life."],[339.83,"And then of these, one that all of us can seek after."],[345.15,"Some people are given special wisdom."],[347.09,"It's one of the gifts, and it is a gift, uh-huh."],[349.65,"But all of us can seek after wisdom, can work to draw closer to God, both in terms of our way of life and in terms of coming to know God in prayer, and through that be able to prophesy in this way, to offer the wisdom of God to other to other people."],[369.3,"This is something we can all pursue."],[371.38,"We may not be all be able to pursue learning languages."],[374.98,"We may not be all be able to pursue wealth and being able to you know give tons of money to give tons of money to charity."],[383.48,"Not successfully, yeah."],[386.12,"And we all have different gifts in terms of like academic learning."],[389.8,"Uh-huh. In terms of what we're good at and what we can remember and and that kind of thing."],[394.04,"But all of us, regardless of education, regardless of our station in life, regardless of gender and and race and class and all those things in St."],[404.04,"Paul's Day, even if you're a slave, you can pursue wisdom by seeking to draw close to God through an aesthetic and disciplined way of life."],[412.76,"Verse 2, for he. who speaks in a tongue does not speak to men but to God, for no one understands him"],[418.8,"However, in the spirit, he speaks mysteries."],[422.56,"That's pretty muddy."],[423.52,"Yeah. So uh if I, if I in the community, he's talking about gifts in the community now."],[432.14,"He's not talking about what I do when I'm at home by myself."],[435.94,"If I in the community pray in a language that no one else understands, God, of course, understands me."],[444.2,"Okay. So if I came into church and just started preaching in Dutch, uh-huh."],[449.94,"right Right? God would know what I was saying."],[453.26,"Probably no one else in the church here would know what I was saying."],[456.46,"And so that wouldn't be that wouldn't be helpful"],[462.78,"Even if even if I'm speaking in the Holy Spirit."],[466.15,"Even if I'm explaining all the mysteries of salvation."],[469.03,"If I'm doing it in a language nobody understands, it's not helping anyone."],[473.99,"It's not beneficial to anyone else."],[476.23,"So he's giving an example of another gift."],[480.07,"My gift may be to speak in a whole bunch of languages."],[483.37,"But I could get up there, I could choose Aramaic, I could choose Eugaritic, I could choose right all kinds of languages I could go up there and say things in, and they might be powerful and profound things, but if no one can understand it, it's not helpful to anyone."],[497.05,"So, however, in the spirit he speaks mysteries, is that should be something more like even if."],[499.53,"That's right Even if."],[503.49,"Okay, yes. Yeah, even if, that's what he's doing."],[508.07,"Uh-huh. You know, then it's still not helpful to anyone, no matter how profound and how good and how powerful what he's saying uh might be."],[517.43,"And that's one way we know that this version of the gift of tongues is not what happened at Pentecost, because in Pentecost, everyone understood."],[526.49,"So in that case, they edified everyone."],[528.49,"That was the whole point of the miracle that took place."],[530.81,"Yeah. Was that they spoke in one language, but everyone, but everyone heard."],[535.13,"Verse three but he who prophesies speaks edification and exhortation and comfort to men."],[541.45,"So being able to speak a bunch of languages right being able to speak a bunch of languages is more impressive probably to people than something that a an uneducated monk might have to say right in terms of what people would would be impressed by uh people are thrown prone to throw in uh Latin phrases you know into what they're saying to make themselves sound smarter right but if people don't really understand what you're saying it doesn't communicate."],[575.95,"Whereas the simple thing that a wise monastic says may truly edify and build up, even though he may not be as impressive, may not look as as sm smartart, may not be book."],[590.83,"What he says is greater than what the person says in a language that's so impressive because of its effect, because of what it does, because of how valuable it is to the to the uh to the body of Christ."],[608.03,"He who speaks in a tongue edifies himself, but he who prophesies edifies the church."],[613.81,"Edifies means to build up or exalt."],[617.11,"So if I'm standing there talking in a bunch of languages no one knows, making all kinds of arcane references and using big words."],[624.73,"I might build myself up."],[626.17,"I might really impress her."],[627.45,"Wow, he's smart."],[629.18,"But that's just me. and probably not a positive thing in the first in terms of my pride and my ego and my arrogance"],[638.18,"But the person who prophesies, the per person who offers this simple wisdom, edifies everyone."],[646.87,"Everyone who is there."],[648.79,"You know, I had no understanding of this idea at all before I came in contact with orthodoxy."],[658.79,"And when I got to when I was reading uh the brothers Karamanzov and got to Father Sosima, who is doing this."],[668.31,"Yeah. Um it was like this revelation, and that's what I think of when I hear this is saints, real ones as well as sexual ones."],[680.05,"Like this. Um I used to read this entirely in terms of well, those churches where they speak in tongues are missing, they're not doing the right thing."],[691.65,"But this is this is totally different from that, and I think if you don't have any cultural experience of um what the Russians would call a starets or a a wise spiritual leader, um then this is not gonna make much sense to you."],[715.01,"Yeah. Uh it certainly didn't to me before."],[719.07,"Yeah, and it's been unfortunately because of, especially in the United States, the influence of the Pentecostal movement and that kind of thing."],[726.15,"Both of these things, both prophecy and speaking in tongues are taken to be these sort of weird, supernatural, miraculous occurrences."],[734.2,"You know, and that makes what St."],[736.28,"Paul is trying to communicate kind of unintelligible."],[739.0,"It's sort of like well, wait, St."],[741.08,"Paul's saying this one weird supernatural thing is better than this other weird supernatural thing, and you should pursue the one and not the other."],[749.0,"I mean, it doesn't make much sense."],[752.01,"At all. Uh, but if we understand, because and he's defined for us a couple chapters back what prophecy is, and he's defined for us what speaking languages means."],[762.82,"And so it's pretty clear"],[766.62,"But this this is this is important and has become increasingly important in our culture."],[773.58,"The Jewish culture and the Roman culture to a somewhat lesser extent, but it's still very much there."],[780.94,"Had this sort of built in respect for the elder, for the older, wiser person."],[789.55,"And that's not an element in our culture today in America at all."],[797.21,"Uh people, if it uh an elderly person today tries to offer wisdom, they get told, okay, boomer."],[803.57,"And told to you know, but you're out old, you're out of touch."],[805.17,"to Yeah, everybody wants seem young and hip and with it and you know, etc."],[808.49,"Uh-huh. You know, everybody wants you to know how to use your cell phone."],[816.09,"etc. The fact that I use those phrases shows that I'm not young and hip and I don't even know what it is anymore, so that I could be with it."],[824.49,"Uh but yeah, that's everyone and everyone spends spends a lot of their time and a lot of their money trying to look and seem younger than they are, you know, and all of it."],[837.13,"So we're oriented in the exact opposite direction."],[840.17,"And that's why what St."],[841.21,"Paul is saying here has become sort of more and more important."],[845.13,"Even when we do break that a little, it's usually just from the step before."],[850.77,"Right now, we sort of worship youth."],[853.41,"Before that, it was that we we sort of worshipped intellect."],[858.05,"Somebody with a PhD, somebody with doctor in front of their name."],[861.25,"Well, they must know what they're talking about."],[863.43,"Oh, this is a scientist."],[865.03,"He, you know, this is an expert."],[868.15,"He knows what he's talking about."],[869.83,"And St. Paul is sort of calling both of those into question."],[873.93,"At the same time."],[875.13,"And saying that it's the person, no matter how simple, no matter how uneducated, could be completely illiterate, could be a slave at his time, but who has spent a life drawing close to God and following God."],[889.45,"This person has words to share with you that will build you you up. up and lift"],[897.09,"Speaking many languages was the mark of education in the ancient world."],[903.81,"That's why St."],[904.77,"Paul is using this as an example."],[907.09,"That was the mark of education was how many languages you were able to speak."],[911.25,"If you only spoke one, you are uneducated."],[916.05,"If you spoke whatever the native language of your place was and you spoke Greek, you were educated."],[922.21,"If you also knew some Latin and some other things, then you would be considered highly educated."],[928.26,"So St. Paul is very much here contrasting wisdom and knowledge."],[934.72,"Wisdom and learning."],[936.56,"And saying that learning, while valuable, he's already listed as one of the gifts from God, and something that the body, the community of the church needs, it needs learned people, it needs scholars to do their job in the service of Christ to serve the body."],[955.48,"But more important is this wisdom that the church receives from its most pious and learned and accomplished members."],[965.16,"Not always monastics, but the monastic life is a way of very intensely pursuing that with one's with one's whole life."],[974.52,"And so St"],[975.6,"Paul is saying if you're going to pursue something, it's better to spend your time pursuing God and drawing closer to Him through your way of life and discipline than to go and try and pursue greater education."],[989.86,"And St. Paul is saying this as someone who is one of the most highly educated people anyone in Corinth would ever meet."],[997.76,"Truly multilingual. Like and thoroughly studied in the scriptures and the traditions of his people, and in Greek and Roman literature, he's the one saying this."],[1010.61,"That what these people have who have drawn close to Christ is more valuable than what I bring to the table as a scholar and a rabbi and a teacher."],[1019.49,"And verse five, I wish you all spoke with tongues, but even more that you prophesied"],[1024.93,"For he who prophesies is greater than he who speaks with tongues, unless indeed he interprets that the church may receive edification."],[1033.06,"So he says, you know, I wish you all could learn other languages."],[1038.68,"Like they could learn Hebrew and read the scriptures, of the original language, then learn more about, you know, the traditions, of the people that they've now been grafted into as converts to Christianity."],[1053.08,"All kinds of reasons why that would be great."],[1054.76,"So I wish you could all do that."],[1056.44,"But more than that, I wish you would all pursue wisdom."],[1061.56,"I wish you would all pursue this prophetic lifestyle."],[1066.36,"Because that will help that will help everyone in the whole community."],[1071.68,"And he makes the obvious point that if somebody speaks languages, that's only helpful if there's somebody else there who understands them."],[1080.48,"Yeah. If there's someone there who could interpret them."],[1084.94,"So if someone were to say, Well, I, you know, I I read this in Eucharitic, but this is what it says and explains it."],[1093.19,"Then he could say, I'm building up other people."],[1095.32,"But still, the person who prophesies is greater than that."],[1098.4,"Is greater than that."],[1099.68,"Verse 6. But now, brethren, if I come to you speaking with tongues, what shall I profit you unless I speak to you either by revelation, by knowledge, by prophesying, or by teaching?"],[1109.34,"So he says, you know, he's furthering the point."],[1112.06,"If I I could come, I could speak a whole bunch of languages."],[1114.86,"St. Paul can speak a whole bunch of languages."],[1116.78,"He says, if I come to them, how is that going to help you."],[1119.6,"Unless I'm revealing something to you, giving you some knowledge, giving you some wisdom, teaching you something."],[1126.8,"Even things without life, whether flute or harp, when they make a sound, unless they make a distinction in the sounds, how little will be known what is piped or played?"],[1136.72,"So he's he's making the point that if someone just if someone comes and speaks to you in a language you don't know, it's like someone just playing one note."],[1147.18,"Just one long note."],[1148.88,"Like a dial tone or a right, or a test test path."],[1153.2,"Well, they don't have those anymore, but for us old folks, a test pattern on the television."],[1156.0,"That's meaningless Just one long sound."],[1161.75,"It's meaningless. There's no meaning to it."],[1164.63,"Whereas if there's variation, it becomes music."],[1167.69,"He says, Well, music is something."],[1169.45,"Yes. Music conveys something."],[1171.47,"But that music, by the fact that it's music, that's what makes it intelligible to you."],[1175.95,"For if the trumpet makes an uncertain sound, who will prepare for battle?"],[1181.45,"So it's sort of like you know, you hear uh somebody playing revelity on a trumpet or something."],[1188.41,"Well, if they just came on the trumpet and went, people will be like, What?"],[1195.15,"What is that?"],[1196.03,"But the fact that they understand the sequence of sounds tells them, oh, uh-huh, time to get up, or time to go to bed, or we're being attacked, or right."],[1208.07,"So likewise, you, unless you utter by the tongue words easy to understand, how will it be known what is spoken?"],[1214.55,"For you will be speaking into the air."],[1217.76,"That's kind of obvious."],[1219.66,"You're talking to no one in particular, if uh no one understands."],[1224.94,"There are, it may be, so many kinds of languages in the world, and none of them is without significance."],[1231.18,"Therefore, if I do not know the meaning of the language, I shall be a foreigner to him who speaks, and he who speaks will be a foreigner to me."],[1238.94,"So this is another place where it makes it clear what kind of languages he's talking about."],[1243.08,"He says there's all kinds of languages in the world."],[1245.32,"This isn't some kind of spiritual gobbledygook."],[1247.82,"This is all kinds of languages of the world."],[1249.74,"They all mean something."],[1253.42,"But if if I don't know what you're saying and you don't know what I'm saying, we're foreigners to each other."],[1260.7,"It's just, oh, that guy's saying some strange foreign gibber jabber."],[1267.47,"Um that's where the word, by the way, that barbarian comes from."],[1272.11,"Is barbari in Greek, because they thought people who didn't speak Greek sounded like bar, bar, bar, bar, bar."],[1280.03,"So they called they called anyone who didn't speak Greek that as a pejorative."],[1285.39,"Even so so. even, you, since you are zealous for spiritual gifts, let it be for the edification of the church that you seek to excel"],[1293.83,"So this is the criteria."],[1296.15,"When you're thinking to yourself, which hopefully people are, what can I do to help the church?"],[1303.12,"Or what should I ask God for?"],[1305.6,"How should I I try to uh follow Christ?"],[1309.28,"St. Paul is saying your focus should be on helping, because again, we're starting from love."],[1314.96,"Love is what makes this all worthwhile."],[1317.52,"Uh, I should be saying, how can I serve the other people here?"],[1323.44,"Not what do I think would be neat or what I like or would make me look good or make me feel important or you know give me a position of prominence, but rather how can I best serve everyone else."],[1336.3,"And unless you happen to be in a community where 12 different languages are spoken, that's probably not gonna be it."],[1346.08,"If you are in such a community, then yeah, learning those 12 languages would be very helpful."],[1351.92,"If I learned Arabic, that would be sometimes useful in this church where so many people speak Arabic."],[1360.48,"Verse 13, therefore, let him who speaks in a tongue pray that he may interpret."],[1365.44,"For if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prays, but my understanding is unfruitful."],[1370.56,"Okay, so he says, Um, if you're going to to use another language, to pray, then you need also interpret it or have it interpreted so other people understand."],[1385.04,"Because if I if I pray in some language no one else knows, yeah, my spirit is praying, I'm praying."],[1391.52,"I may be truly praying, I may be truly drawing close to God, but my me understanding what I'm saying is not fruitful."],[1400.64,"It doesn't produce any kind of benefit, any kind of fruit in the community."],[1405.6,"What is the conclusion then?"],[1407.44,"I will pray with the spirit and I will also pray with understanding."],[1411.84,"I will sing with the spirit, and I will also sing with understanding."],[1415.76,"Otherwise, if you bless with the spirit, how will he who occupies the place of the uninformed say amen at your giving of thanks?"],[1423.48,"Since he does not understand what you say."],[1426.37,"Now remember, Amen, of course, they would say at the end of prayers just as we do, but what it means is basically truly."],[1432.15,"It's a way of expressing agreement."],[1436.79,"Say, well, how can everyone else agree with your prayer if they don't know what you said?"],[1441.69,"You might have said all kinds of horrible things."],[1444.49,"This sounds like the custom was that there was some circumstance under which individuals would stand up and pray in front of the channel."],[1457.11,"There were people who were leading the worship."],[1459.59,"Uh-huh. Okay, so this leading and presiding. in worship, yeah"],[1463.63,"Um did they often speak in languages that people didn't understand?"],[1469.95,"Well, there were they were in multilingual communities."],[1474.43,"Uh-huh. And I think I mentioned this a little last time, but it bears repeating."],[1479.31,"We we uh don't realize how multilingual the Roman Empire was, and how multilingual a lot of the areas where St."],[1489.71,"Paul preached were."],[1492.19,"Um that essentially all of all of these areas that had been annexed by the Roman Empire, at this time Greek was the common language."],[1504.03,"But of course, even in Greek you've got dialects."],[1506.43,"It differences in different regions"],[1509.81,"But beyond that, all of these regions had local languages too."],[1514.53,"We're probably most familiar with in Judea and Galilee, people were speaking Aramaic."],[1519.73,"But in Egypt, people were speaking late Egyptian, which later became Coptic as a written language."],[1526.21,"They were speaking a version of Aramaic in Syria that became Syriac, but it's a different dialect of Aramaic."],[1532.21,"But there were local languages in Asia Minor, what's now Turkey, that a lot of the Galatian Christians would have spoken."],[1539.89,"And the common people, the people who weren't Roman citizens, their Greek would be probably Iffy."],[1546.05,"There's an instance in St."],[1547.33,"John's Gospel where some Greeks come to see Jesus, and they go to uh St."],[1551.49,"Andrew Philip and St."],[1558.97,"And this is probably not coincidental because St."],[1561.53,"Philip and St."],[1562.17,"Andrew are both from Galilee, but also both have Greek names."],[1565.61,"So they were probably the two uh disciples who knew Greek the best."],[1573.18,"Out of all the disciples."],[1574.86,"You know, saying that Greek was the common language, just like in the United States, English is the common language, but we have a lot of people here who are immigrants and have varying level of skill in English."],[1589.62,"Well, a good example would be India, where English is the common language throughout the country, but people most people don't actually know English."],[1595.74,"Yeah They know varying amounts."],[1605.78,"Yeah. Yeah. Anywhere you go from a couple words to relative fluency."],[1611.3,"Yes. Uh-huh. Lots of people are very fluent, but the poorest people won't be."],[1616.26,"They will only know the local language, which as in the Roman Empire varies all over the place."],[1622.43,"And so, and then you also have to take into account we've got a large number of slaves from all kinds of different who have been brought to all kinds of different places from all kinds of different places."],[1638.27,"So you may have Egyptian slaves in Corinth or from other African countries or from the Middle East or from somewhere in your Germanic slaves who wouldn't necessarily know much Greek or any of whatever the native language is."],[1656.41,"If you've got a Germanic slave in Egypt, he's not going to know any Egyptian."],[1661.37,"He might know some Greek, but so if whoever's presiding at the Eucharist is speaking Greek in Corinth, that doesn't necessarily, especially depending on his Greek."],[1680.01,"If he's highly fluent, if he's speaking sort of high-level traditional, you know, the equivalent in Greek of King James English."],[1689.03,"Then someone who's there who Greek is their third or fourth language might not have any idea what he's saying."],[1698.48,"Yeah, and so it's entirely possible that there would be situations like that."],[1703.6,"Uh and so St."],[1705.28,"Paul is saying if if we're in that sort of situation and you're leading the prayers, you need to either you need to interpret if you're multilingual."],[1714.45,"Or you need someone else to interpret for those people so that everyone knows everyone knows what's going on."],[1721.89,"So yeah, the whole language thing is not just sort of a modern orthodox problem in the United States."],[1729.13,"This is a problem where ever Christian communities get set up and people speak different languages, which to one degree or another affects most of the world."],[1739.85,"So verse 17, for you indeed give thanks well, but the other is not edified."],[1744.57,"Now, of course, give thanks is literally could be translated Eucharistize."],[1752.42,"You Eucharistize very well."],[1754.1,"So he's talking about worship."],[1755.7,"He's talking about what's going on in worship."],[1757.4,"So he's so he's basically saying, you know, yes, your presbyter who led who presided at the Eucharist that day did it very beautifully in Greek."],[1769.61,"But if half of the people there have no idea what just happened, what was the what was the purpose?"],[1777.17,"And this is this understanding is why the Orthodox Church has always had such a tradition of translation, including creating languages like Coptic and like the Cyrillic alphabet for Eastern Europe and the Slavic countries and for for Russia, um and translating the scriptures and the liturgy and that kind of thing."],[1800.29,"Uh because it's embedded here with St."],[1802.93,"Paul asking that question."],[1804.29,"If no one understands what you're doing, you haven't, you haven't edified them, you haven't built them up, you haven't helped them draw closer to Christ."],[1812.69,"So this would be this would criticize liturgical languages that are not the languages of the people."],[1820.97,"Yeah, that people don't know."],[1822.83,"Unless there's some interpretation available."],[1824.99,"Uh-huh. Some some education to bring them to that knowledge."],[1829.98,"Verse 18, I thank my God I speak with tongues more than you all."],[1834.94,"Yet in the church I would rather speak five words with my understanding, that I may teach others also, than ten thousand words in a tongue."],[1844.38,"Now, this is another place where I I would think it would be obvious what St."],[1848.14,"Paul is talking about."],[1849.1,"When he says, I am, says he thanks God that he sp he speaks more languages than all of them."],[1855.42,"He speaks with more languages than all of them."],[1858.0,"I know there are people who interpret this as, oh, this means he speaks the spiritual gobbledygook more often than the rest of them."],[1859.76,"Yeah, that's how I always thought it meant"],[1866.76,"First of all, first of all, how would he know that?"],[1869.08,"Uh-huh. But secondly, the way he develops that."],[1875.66,"Yet in the church, I would rather speak five words with my understanding."],[1880.2,"I'd rather speak five words."],[1882.2,"Five words with understanding."],[1884.28,"Now, you could, I suppose, interpret this as he's saying the spiritual gobbledygook, and even he doesn't understand what he's saying."],[1891.72,"But then that would contradict what he said a few verses earlier that said, When I pray in a tongue, I understand."],[1898.68,"But others don't."],[1899.81,"So that isn't possible either."],[1901.51,"So the point is is very clear."],[1902.87,"St. Sa Payul's saying, I speak more languages than any of you, which he safely we could say he does."],[1910.21,"He's probably more highly educated than anyone else there, or at least as highly educated."],[1915.73,"He says, I speak more languages than all of you."],[1918.77,"So if we're gonna brag about languages, knowing a lot of languages, I've got you all beat."],[1923.91,"He said, But in the church, when I'm in the community, he may at home pray in whatever language he wants."],[1930.17,"You know? But in the church, he'd rather say five words that people understand so that other people can learn, so that he can teach."],[1939.5,"Then say 10,000 words in some language they don't understand that might impress them."],[1945.04,"But would not bear any fruit."],[1945.52,"not. Brethren, do be children in understanding"],[1952.28,"However, in malice be babes, but in understanding be mature."],[1957.7,"So he's saying he's he's hearkening back to themes from the beginning of the epistle."],[1963.38,"Remember, he said, you know, I uh you still need milk, but you want solid food."],[1969.64,"He talked about their maturity back at the beginning, that they're being very immature."],[1974.3,"So he says, I don't want you to be immature, I don't want you to be children in terms of your understanding."],[1979.66,"He says, in malice you should be like a baby, you should be innocent."],[1984.75,"Although babies can be rather malicious when they want to."],[1987.79,"But in understanding, be mature"],[1992.15,"He says, In the law it is written, with men of other tongues and other lips, I will speak to this people, and yet for all that they will not hear me, says the Lord."],[2002.31,"Now again, when St."],[2003.67,"Paul quotes this, could this is this talking about spiritual gobbledygook?"],[2009.11,"No. Where's it?"],[2010.31,"Where's this from?"],[2011.03,"What's the quotation from?"],[2012.71,"That uh quotation is from Isaiah."],[2014.63,"He says, With men of other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people, and yet for all that they will not hear me."],[2020.57,"He's talking about men' ofs other men of other tongues and other lips would be people from other nations."],[2027.59,"I'll speak to this people, that would be the people of Israel. who Isaiah is prophesying to, but they will not hear me"],[2037.47,"And so he's talking about this was this was prophesied that people from all nations and all languages would come and would speak."],[2046.75,"And so he's talking about, he hasn't departed from this theme of love."],[2051.69,"And of the oneness of the community."],[2054.65,"Because even though this community is mainly Gentile, you got all different kinds of Gentiles from all different nations."],[2061.77,"And that unity means we need to hear one another, which means we need to understand each other, which means we need to communicate in ways that we can all that we can all understand together."],[2074.49,"And this is this is keeping with. even the themes from the previous section about worship"],[2082.77,"Why why would someone we've we've talked about ego."],[2088.47,"Is one reason why someone might want to show off that they speak a bunch of languages."],[2092.72,"To exalt themselves."],[2094.53,"There's another reason, though, also, and that's related to what we talked about in the previous section."],[2099.49,"You have to remember how worship was seen by these folks when they were pagans."],[2105.89,"The people in the community at Corinth, the people in the church at Corinth who were former pagans, remember the previous section was all about trying to get them to change their ideas of what worship was."],[2116.79,"In terms of the pagan sacrifices, the pagan feasts, and the sexuality that was part of the pagan rituals."],[2122.93,"We need to separate from that."],[2125.7,"But we also have to remember the existence in Greco Roman religion, and of almost all pagan religion, of magic."],[2134.15,"Of ritual magic."],[2136.31,"Where they believed that performing a ritual correctly and saying sort of the correct magic words in the correct language, whether you understood them or not, made things happen."],[2152.76,"Made caused things to happen."],[2155.96,"And so priests in the Greco Roman world, are not priests like we. think of them"],[2163.46,"We think of priests as being because it's been informed by Christian priests that priests or pastors or ministers in the Protestant world, are sort of community leaders who are primarily teachers."],[2177.57,"Teachers and preachers and guys."],[2179.41,"But we're getting that from Saint Paul."],[2182.05,"In fact, from this passage in St."],[2184.13,"Paul. Priests of the ancient world were ritual experts."],[2189.11,"Their job was ritual minutiae."],[2192.47,"Knowing all the little details of how to do the rituals correctly in order to get the gods to do what you want."],[2199.59,"Yeah. And it was a very strange."],[2201.99,"Very strange ones."],[2203.27,"And trying to get the the cor correrectct ones."],[2207.63,"So they would have they would have spoken in magic words."],[2213.71,"Yes. Yeah. There would be words that probably didn't make sense in any language, but which had or were from some archaic form of some language that they no longer understood."],[2225.53,"More likely. Yeah."],[2226.73,"And for example, a friend of mine, this is the kind of friends I have, who was recently reading a bunch of ancient Greek magical texts."],[2235.21,"Told me he found one for how to, if you're if you're going to overindulge in alcohol and you don't want to get a hangover, uh, the way to prevent that was to say these certain words uh to one of the gods and then eat the boiled lung of a sheep of a pig, sorry, boiled lung of a pig."],[2250.33,"and Boil the lung of a pig eat it after saying these words, and then you can drink as much as you want and not get a hangover."],[2262.77,"Wow. Was the theory."],[2265.81,"When you say, yeah, when you say bizarre rituals, there you go."],[2269.73,"Um, but even on the uh it's still bizarre to us, but uh just a mundane everyday thing, one of the things we find all the time at ritual sites archaeologically, are these sort of scale models of sheep livers and hearts and and other organs that have all these markings on them that are telling you when you kill the animal to sacrifice it, and you're butchering it, you'd look at the organs for these signs that would help you predict the future and primarily let you know it was called extipacy, and the priest who did it was called a haruspex."],[2306.57,"And it would would let you know whether the gods were going to accept this sacrifice or not."],[2313.11,"So there's there's one famous case recorded before a Roman battle where they killed an animal, I believe it was a bull, and when they were butchering it, they it had no heart."],[2323.27,"Or at least they couldn't find it."],[2324.71,"But uh it had no heart, and so this was this horrible omen of doom."],[2329.16,"That the the sacrifice was not accepted and they were gonna lose the battle, which of course they did."],[2333.56,"But so it was the the priests were involved in that kind of thing."],[2337.96,"That's what a priest did."],[2339.4,"And Saint Paul here, even though it may not resonate with us right away, because we already take for granted what St."],[2348.0,"Paul is teaching, is changing what the role of a presbyter in the community is."],[2355.36,"He's changing what the role of the person who presides, whether it's an episcopus, a bishop, a presbyter, a priest, or a uh diaconos, a deacon, what the role of the leadership in the church is and how they function."],[2370.16,"And it's not to be this sort of ritual expert who reads all these things and who says all the words perfectly and in the right order, so that God will do what He wanted we want him to do and give us crops and blessings and whatever. else it is"],[2385.96,"And of course, with this kind of magic, if what you want to have happen doesn't happen, you know that you did something wrong."],[2393.7,"You said the wrong word or we didn't do what we were supposed to do."],[2400.24,"This is this and this is the this is central to Roman religion in particular."],[2406.16,"They famously had the Sibyllean oracles, these books that had been written by the Sybil, this prophetess, in sort of ancient Roman prehistory."],[2416.8,"And no one could see these books except for this one priesthood who had them underneath a temple on Jupiter's Hill in Rome."],[2424.96,"And the whole job of that priesthood was to protect these books, and whenever there was a major crisis in Roman life, to go and look up in these books, because everything important was prophesied, and see, okay, what are we supposed to do to avert this disaster?"],[2443.96,"And this would be again bizarre things, like they buried a couple of slaves alive when Hannibal was coming across the Alps, because that's what it told them to do."],[2453.82,"They'd see a comet in the sky."],[2455.5,"Hey, tell those people what do we need to do?"],[2457.66,"It's a bad omen."],[2459.44,"Go look at the books."],[2460.48,"So this is how priests function."],[2462.8,"Yeah, later on, uh, these books were supposed to were interpreted to have foretold the coming of Christ."],[2470.02,"And they were ultimately, in terms of what happened to those books, they were burned in 430 AD when Rome was under attack."],[2480.3,"A bunch of the Romans in Rome decided that the reason the gods were angry and were bringing all these barbarians to pillage them was that they'd converted to Christianity and made the gods mad."],[2492.7,"And so they wanted to consult the books to find out what they needed to do to get the gods back on their side and try to bring back paganism."],[2500.54,"So the Christian governor of Rome publicly burned them in front of everybody and said there's no going back to paganism."],[2508.14,"But so, yeah, so this is how priesthood was conceived."],[2511.98,"This is how a religious functionary was conceived."],[2515.1,"And St. Paul is reorienting that to know this is a per the leader needs to be the person who pursues prophecy, who pursues this way of life, who pursues this wisdom, and try is trying to build up and teach and instruct and offer wisdom to the whole body."],[2531.56,"To the whole community."],[2533.08,"That's what leadership of the church looks like, not this sort of esoteric weird magical stuff."],[2539.8,"I remember one weird thing was that the fingernail clippings of the priest of Jupiter had to be preserved."],[2546.76,"Yeah. And couldn't be thrown away."],[2550.04,"Yeah, yeah. There's all manner of."],[2554.06,"And so he's one of the things, again, St."],[2556.46,"Paul's having to do is change that understanding."],[2559.8,"And he's doing it. it through here, he's attack he's attacked in a whole bunch of different ways all through First Corinthians"],[2566.48,"Here he's attacking it through the understanding of language and how language works in worship."],[2573.28,"That the way language works in worship is to build everyone up, to teach, to instruct, to offer wisdom, to edify everyone."],[2581.37,"That's the way language works in worship."],[2584.01,"Not in this sort of mystical way where it doesn't matter if anyone understands it, because it does what it's supposed to do."],[2592.33,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2599.69,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancient faith.com."],[2604.89,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":47.12,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where we left off","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at start of <b>1 Corinthians 14</b>; intro podcast now online</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's phrase <b>\"now concerning...\"</b> separates topics</li><li class=\"d1\">current topic from ch. 12: <b>spiritual gifts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So far</li><li class=\"d1\">analogy of the <b>body</b> = community, many parts, all necessary</li><li class=\"d1\">examples of particular gifts</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>love</b> for one another: rejoice together, sorrow together → unity</li><li class=\"d1\">most gifted person without love is <b>not useful</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last argument: day coming when <b>Christ returns</b> - languages, wisdom, education, wealth all <b>go away</b></li><li class=\"d1\">love continues forever → <b>ultimately superior</b></li></ul>"},{"t":217.56,"lv":2,"h":"14:1 - Pursue love, desire prophecy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:1</b>: pursue love, desire spiritual gifts, <b>especially that you may prophesy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sums up: love first, then gifts</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophecy not predicting future, not a mystical revelation</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not like <i>The Dead Zone</i>\" - Christopher Walken handshake vision</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 12: prophecy tied to <b>spiritual wisdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Corinthians ex-pagans had mystery rituals promising supernatural wisdom → Paul: <b>from the Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Prophetic life of OT → <b>monastic life</b> in new covenant; <b>John the Baptist</b> the pivot</li><li class=\"d0\">Desire to prophesy = that <b>lifestyle</b> and wisdom</li></ul>"},{"t":322.67,"lv":3,"h":"Q: asceticism? Wisdom open to all","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: does Paul mean <b>asceticism</b>? Yes - seeking to draw close to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Number one is <b>love</b>; then seek God's gifts</li><li class=\"d0\">Some given special wisdom as a <b>gift</b>, but <b>all can seek</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">draw closer through <b>way of life</b> and <b>prayer</b> → offer God's wisdom to others</li><li class=\"d0\">Not all can pursue languages, wealth for charity, academic learning</li><li class=\"d0\">Regardless of education, station, gender, race, class - even a <b>slave</b> can pursue wisdom through an <b>ascetic, disciplined life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":412.76,"lv":2,"h":"14:2 - Speaking in a tongue","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:2</b>: speaks not to men but to God; no one understands; in the spirit speaks <b>mysteries</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gifts <b>in the community</b>, not at home alone</li><li class=\"d0\">Praying in a language no one understands - God understands</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if I came in and started preaching in <b>Dutch</b>\" - God knows, church doesn't → not helpful</li><li class=\"d0\">Even speaking in the Spirit, explaining mysteries of salvation → <b>not beneficial</b> if not understood</li><li class=\"d1\">gift of many languages: choose Aramaic, <b>Ugaritic</b> - profound but unhelpful</li><li class=\"d0\">Better: \"<b>even if</b> in the spirit he speaks mysteries\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not Pentecost version: at Pentecost <b>everyone understood</b> - edified all</li></ul>"},{"t":535.13,"lv":2,"h":"14:3-4 - Prophecy edifies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:3</b>: prophesies = <b>edification, exhortation, comfort</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Many languages more impressive than an <b>uneducated monk</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like people throwing in <b>Latin phrases</b> to sound smarter</li><li class=\"d1\">simple word of a wise monastic truly <b>builds up</b> - greater by its <b>effect</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:4</b>: tongue edifies himself; prophet edifies the church</li><li class=\"d1\">edify = <b>build up</b></li><li class=\"d1\">arcane references, big words → build myself up, \"Wow, he's smart\" - pride and ego</li><li class=\"d1\">simple wisdom edifies <b>everyone</b> there</li></ul>"},{"t":648.79,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: the <i>starets</i> and Pentecostal influence","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Comment: no idea before Orthodoxy - <i>Brothers Karamazov</i>, <b>Fr Zosima</b></li><li class=\"d1\">used to read it as \"churches that speak in tongues are missing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">needs cultural experience of a <b>starets</b>, wise spiritual leader</li><li class=\"d0\">US <b>Pentecostal</b> influence: prophecy and tongues both read as weird supernatural occurrences</li><li class=\"d1\">then Paul's comparison unintelligible - \"one weird thing better than another\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but he defined both terms already</li></ul>"},{"t":766.62,"lv":3,"h":"Respect for elders, youth and intellect","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jewish and Roman culture: built-in <b>respect for the older, wiser</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not in America today</li><li class=\"d1\">elderly offering wisdom: \"<b>okay, boomer</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone wants to seem <b>young and hip</b>; spend time and money looking younger</li><li class=\"d0\">Before: we worshipped <b>intellect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"doctor in front of the name\", scientist, expert</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul calls <b>both into question</b></li><li class=\"d1\">simple, uneducated, even <b>illiterate slave</b> who spent a life drawing close to God has words that <b>build you up</b></li></ul>"},{"t":897.09,"lv":3,"h":"Languages as the mark of education","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In ancient world, <b>many languages = education</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one language = uneducated; local + Greek = educated; plus Latin = <b>highly educated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul contrasts <b>wisdom and learning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">learning is a <b>gift</b>, church needs scholars</li><li class=\"d1\">but more important: <b>wisdom</b> from its most pious members</li><li class=\"d1\">not always monastics - monastic life pursues it <b>intensely</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Better to pursue God than greater education</li><li class=\"d1\">said by one of the <b>most educated</b> people in Corinth - multilingual, scriptures, Greek and Roman literature</li><li class=\"d1\">what Christ-drawn people have > what I bring as <b>scholar and rabbi</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1019.49,"lv":2,"h":"14:5 - I wish you all spoke with tongues","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:5</b>: wish all spoke with tongues, <b>even more that you prophesied</b>; unless he interprets</li><li class=\"d0\">Wish they could learn <b>Hebrew</b>, read Scripture in the original, traditions of people they're <b>grafted into</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But more: pursue <b>wisdom, prophetic lifestyle</b> - helps whole community</li><li class=\"d0\">Languages only helpful if someone <b>interprets</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I read this in Ugaritic, this is what it says\" → building up others</li><li class=\"d1\">still prophecy is <b>greater</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1099.68,"lv":2,"h":"14:6-9 - Flute, harp and trumpet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:6</b>: if I come speaking in tongues, what profit unless revelation, knowledge, prophesying, teaching?</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>can</b> speak many languages - how does it help you?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:7</b>: flute or harp without distinct sounds - how known what is played?</li><li class=\"d1\">one long note like a <b>dial tone</b> or <b>test pattern</b> - meaningless</li><li class=\"d1\">variation = <b>music</b>; conveys something, intelligible</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:8</b>: uncertain trumpet - who prepares for battle?</li><li class=\"d1\">reveille: sequence tells you get up, go to bed, <b>being attacked</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:9</b>: unless words easy to understand → \"<b>speaking into the air</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1224.94,"lv":3,"h":"14:10-11 - Foreigners; barbarians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:10-11</b>: so many kinds of languages, none without significance; else I'm a <b>foreigner</b> to the speaker</li><li class=\"d0\">Another place clarifying the kind of languages: <b>languages of the world</b>, not \"spiritual gobbledygook\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Foreign gibber jabber\"</li><li class=\"d1\">source of <b>barbarian</b>: Greek <i>barbaroi</i> - \"bar bar bar bar\"</li><li class=\"d1\">used of anyone not speaking Greek, as pejorative</li></ul>"},{"t":1285.39,"lv":2,"h":"14:12 - Seek to excel for the church's edification","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:12</b>: zealous for gifts → seek to excel for <b>edification of the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The criteria when asking \"what can I do to help the church?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">focus on <b>helping</b> - starting from love</li><li class=\"d1\">\"how can I <b>serve</b> everyone else\", not what's neat, makes me look good, gives prominence</li><li class=\"d0\">Unless community has <b>12 languages</b> spoken, probably not languages</li><li class=\"d1\">learning Arabic useful in church with many Arabic speakers</li></ul>"},{"t":1360.48,"lv":2,"h":"14:13-16 - Pray with spirit and understanding","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:13</b>: pray in a tongue → pray that he may <b>interpret</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:14</b>: my spirit prays, my understanding <b>unfruitful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">may truly draw close to God, but no fruit in the community</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:15</b>: pray, sing <b>with spirit and with understanding</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:16</b>: how will the uninformed say <b>\"Amen\"</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">Amen = <b>\"truly\"</b>, agreement</li><li class=\"d1\">how can others agree if they don't know what you said? \"might have said horrible things\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: sounds like individuals stood to pray / lead worship - did they speak languages people didn't understand?</li></ul>"},{"t":1469.95,"lv":3,"h":"A multilingual empire","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Roman Empire much more <b>multilingual</b> than we realize</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Greek</b> the common language, with dialects; plus local languages</li><li class=\"d1\">Judea, Galilee: <b>Aramaic</b>; Egypt: late Egyptian → <b>Coptic</b>; Syria: Aramaic dialect → <b>Syriac</b>; Asia Minor local languages (Galatians)</li><li class=\"d0\">Common people, non-citizens: Greek <b>iffy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">John's Gospel: Greeks go to <b>Philip and Andrew</b> - both Galileans with Greek names, best Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">Like English in US or <b>India</b> - varying fluency; poorest know only local language</li><li class=\"d0\">Slaves from everywhere: Germanic slave in Egypt knows no Egyptian</li><li class=\"d0\">Presider speaking <b>high-level Greek</b> (\"King James English\") → third-language speaker lost</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: interpret yourself, or have someone interpret → not just a modern Orthodox problem</li></ul>"},{"t":1739.85,"lv":2,"h":"14:17 - Eucharistize well, others not edified","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:17</b>: \"you give thanks well\" = <b>Eucharistize</b> - about worship</li><li class=\"d0\">Presbyter presides beautifully in Greek, half have no idea → \"what was the purpose?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Orthodoxy has a tradition of <b>translation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">created <b>Coptic</b> and the <b>Cyrillic alphabet</b> for Slavic lands, Russia</li><li class=\"d1\">translating Scripture and liturgy</li><li class=\"d0\">If no one understands → <b>not edified</b>, not drawn closer to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">criticizes liturgical languages not of the people - <b>unless interpretation or education</b> is given</li></ul>"},{"t":1829.98,"lv":2,"h":"14:18-19 - Five words with understanding","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:18</b>: \"I speak with tongues more than you all\"; in church <b>five words</b> with understanding than <b>ten thousand</b> in a tongue</li><li class=\"d0\">Some read: Paul speaks \"gobbledygook\" more often</li><li class=\"d1\">how would he know that?</li><li class=\"d1\">contradicts <b>14:14</b> - when I pray in a tongue I understand</li><li class=\"d0\">Clear point: Paul speaks more <b>languages</b> than all - \"I've got you all beat\"</li><li class=\"d1\">probably as <b>highly educated</b> as anyone there</li><li class=\"d0\">At home pray in any language; in church <b>five words</b> to teach rather than 10,000 that impress and bear no fruit</li></ul>"},{"t":1945.52,"lv":2,"h":"14:20 - Be mature in understanding","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:20</b>: don't be children in understanding; in <b>malice</b> be babes, in understanding <b>mature</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Back to themes from start of epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you need milk, want solid food\" - <b>immaturity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Babes in malice = <b>innocent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"babies can be rather malicious when they want to\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1992.15,"lv":2,"h":"14:21 - Isaiah: men of other tongues","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>14:21</b>: \"In the law it is written, with men of other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people, yet they will not hear me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Quotation from <b>Isaiah</b> - not gobbledygook</li><li class=\"d1\">other tongues/lips = people from <b>other nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"this people\" = <b>Israel</b>, they won't hear</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophesied: people from all nations and languages would come and speak</li><li class=\"d0\">Still the theme: <b>love and oneness</b> of the community</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles from all nations - unity means we <b>hear one another</b> = understand each other</li></ul>"},{"t":2082.77,"lv":3,"h":"Magic and ritual in pagan worship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ego one reason to show off languages; another: how <b>pagans saw worship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Previous section: change ideas of worship - <b>sacrifices, feasts, sexuality</b> in pagan rituals</li><li class=\"d0\">Greco-Roman religion = <b>ritual magic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">right ritual + right words in right language, understood or not → <b>made things happen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Priests not like ours</li><li class=\"d1\">our picture: community leaders, <b>teachers and preachers</b> - \"getting that from St. Paul, from this passage\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient priests = <b>ritual experts</b>, minutiae to get the gods to do what you want</li><li class=\"d0\">Magic words: no sense in any language, <b>archaic forms</b> no longer understood</li><li class=\"d1\">friend reading Greek magical texts: <b>hangover</b> cure - words to a god, eat <b>boiled pig lung</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2269.73,"lv":3,"h":"Omens and organ models","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ritual sites: <b>scale models of sheep livers, hearts</b> with markings</li><li class=\"d1\">butchering the sacrifice, read organs to <b>predict the future</b> and whether gods accepted</li><li class=\"d0\">Famous case before a Roman battle: bull with <b>no heart</b> → omen of doom; <b>they lost</b></li><li class=\"d0\">That's what a priest did</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul changes role of <b>presbyter</b>, <i>episcopus</i> (bishop), <i>diaconos</i> (deacon)</li><li class=\"d1\">not ritual expert saying all the words perfectly so God gives crops and blessings</li><li class=\"d1\">with magic, if it fails you <b>did something wrong</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2400.24,"lv":3,"h":"Sibylline books","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Central to <b>Roman religion</b>: the <b>Sibylline oracles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">books by the Sibyl in Roman prehistory; one priesthood kept them under <b>Jupiter's Hill</b> temple</li><li class=\"d1\">in crisis, look up what to do - comet = bad omen, go look</li><li class=\"d1\">buried a couple of <b>slaves alive</b> when <b>Hannibal</b> crossed the Alps</li><li class=\"d0\">Later read as foretelling Christ's coming</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Burned 430 AD</b> when Rome under attack</li><li class=\"d1\">Romans blamed conversion to Christianity; <b>Christian governor</b> burned them publicly - \"no going back to paganism\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2508.14,"lv":2,"h":"Paul reorients priesthood and worship language","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">How a religious functionary was conceived → Paul reorients</li><li class=\"d1\">leader = one who <b>pursues prophecy</b>, this way of life, wisdom</li><li class=\"d1\">builds up, teaches, instructs, offers wisdom to the <b>whole community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not esoteric weird magical stuff\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: priest of Jupiter's <b>fingernail clippings</b> had to be preserved</li><li class=\"d0\">Attacking it all through 1 Corinthians; here through <b>language in worship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Language in worship works to <b>build everyone up</b>, teach, instruct, edify</li><li class=\"d1\">not mystical, where it doesn't matter if anyone understands</li></ul>"}]});
